{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"21f64fcc-5131-4f07-9f0b-ee3ec2549b84","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A cubic can have a local peak and valley. Let’s find both for this function, using its slope.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Local maxima and minima"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^3-3x"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.4,2.4],"y":[-4.6,4.6],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"f(x)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"cubic","y":"x^3-3*x","domain":[-2.2,2.2]}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"21f64fcc-5131-4f07-9f0b-ee3ec2549b84","step":{"id":"s2","script":"At a smooth local extremum, the tangent is horizontal. Setting the derivative equal to zero gives two candidates: minus one and one.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"derivative","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f'(x)=3x^2-3=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=\\pm1"},{"op":"figure","id":"stationary","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"peak","at":{"on":"cubic","x":-1},"label":"(-1, 2)"},{"kind":"point","id":"valley","at":{"on":"cubic","x":1},"label":"(1, -2)"},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":-1,"length":0.25,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":1,"length":0.25,"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"21f64fcc-5131-4f07-9f0b-ee3ec2549b84","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"21f64fcc-5131-4f07-9f0b-ee3ec2549b84","step":{"id":"s3","script":"At these stationary points, the second derivative determines the type: negative gives a local maximum; positive gives a local minimum.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"curvature","anchor":{"el":"derivative","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f''(x)=6x,\\quad f''(-1)=-6<0\\;\\text{(maximum)},\\quad f''(1)=6>0\\;\\text{(minimum)}"},{"op":"figure","id":"classify","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[-1.05,3.05],"text":"local max","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.05,-3.05],"text":"local min","size":"small"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"21f64fcc-5131-4f07-9f0b-ee3ec2549b84","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Local means compared with nearby points, not the entire curve. Farther right the cubic rises higher; farther left it falls lower.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"beyond","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"cubic","x":2.2}},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"cubic","x":-2.2}}]},{"op":"equation","id":"results","anchor":{"el":"curvature","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\text{Local max: }(-1,2)\\qquad\\text{Local min: }(1,-2)"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Is the local maximum at x = -1 also the greatest value of this cubic everywhere?","expected_gist":"No; the cubic grows without bound as x increases."}}}
